.TH GORGE 1
.SH NAME
gorge \- fly a canyon, in real 3-D
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/gorge
[
.B -t
|
.B -b
|
.B -s
|
.B -g
] [
.B -q
] [
.B -S
|
.B -D
] [
.B -n
.I frames
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gorge
is the other half of the argument the four games beside it make.
.IR Rivet (1),
.IR sprocket (1),
.IR starling (1)
and
.IR orchard (1)
are 8-bit in shape:
.IR tiles (2)
composites tiles once at the destination's scale and every frame is
blits into an offscreen surface.
That is deliberate, and it is also what
.B doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
names as getting no GPU at all.
.PP
.I Gorge
has no tiles and no palette.
The canyon is geometry \- a floor and two walls, one quad per segment,
about two hundred faces a frame \- lit per vertex and handed to
.IR draw3d (2)'s
.I fillpoly3g
straight into the window image, which is the only destination
.IR emu (1)'s
Metal backend will take.
Colour is whatever the display's own channels can express.
The brightness ramp down the corridor is what makes a Gouraud fill worth
having: the same scene flat-shaded per face bands visibly at every
segment boundary.
.PP
The one thing here that is a sprite and not geometry is the explosion,
and it is a sprite on purpose:
.I sprite3zb
turns a flat image to face the eye and depth-tests it against everything
already drawn, so a burst behind a pylon is hidden by it.
The image is built at startup rather than opened, so this game carries
no asset file at all.
.PP
Fly with the arrow keys or
.BR wasd ,
fire with
.BR x ,
.B .
or the space bar.
Shooting a pylon scores 150 and flying past one scores 25; hitting one
costs a ship.
The canyon gets faster the further you get.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/gorge.dis
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/gorge.dis \-b
.EE
.PP
Run it as above and not under
.IR wm (1);
see BUGS.
.SS How smooth, not just how fast
.B -b
runs unpaced \- the number wanted is what a frame costs, and pacing is
what hides it \- and times every frame rather than the run as a whole,
because a mean says nothing about smoothness: sixty frames of two
milliseconds and one of two hundred average out to something that reads
fine and looks like a lurch.
It reports the spread, the tail, how many frames were slower than a
sixtieth of a second, and a histogram.
Three hundred frames on an Apple M-series host:
.IP
.EX
                mean   min  median   p90    p99    max   over
GPU             2204  1665    1988  2872   5354   7932    0
devdraw C       1961  1381    1539  2733   7320   8838    0
software       62179 56072   61683 69270  77048  77048   60
.EE
.PP
All microseconds.
.B over
is frames past 16666.
The two fast paths never miss a frame and their worst is about half the
budget; the software provider misses every one of them, by up to four
and a half times.
.PP
The same numbers for
.IR strider (1),
which draws rather more:
mean 3237, median 2797, p99 8696, worst 9347 \- again nothing past the
budget.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/gorge.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw3dbench (1),
.IR tiles (2),
.IR rivet (1)
.SS Looking at what the GPU actually drew
.B -g
plays ninety frames into the window, reads the window back, and writes
it to
.B gorge.gpu.raw
as 32-bit BGRX, reporting how many pixels are not sky and how many are
white.
The white count is the head-up display:
.IP
.EX
games/gorge -g -S   -> non-sky 289920 of 290928, HUD pixels 1277
games/gorge -g -D   -> non-sky 290559 of 290928, HUD pixels 2047
.EE
.PP
Both draw the canyon, the ceiling, the pylons, the bolt and the whole
head-up display.
That was not true until 2026-09-11: the device provider drew a frame
with no HUD at all and a mis-composed canyon, and three faults in
.IR emu ,
none of them in this program, were behind it.
See
.B /doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
and
.IR g3probe (1).
.PP
The device provider is the default as of that date, and
.B -S
asks for the software one.
It has been run under a window manager too, which is where it used to
panic
.I emu
with
.BR "pushrun addrun" :
three runs of this and two of
.IR strider (1)
through
.IR wm (1),
no panic, 1675 to 2416 microseconds a frame.
That panic was an unbalanced error stack in
.IR devdraw 's
own Gouraud fallback.
.PP
.B -t
and
.B -s
still render through the software provider: they draw into an offscreen
image, which the GPU hook does not take, and they are checks rather than
play.
